$t$ (for $m \\ge 1$, its expansion in powers…","route":"../roadmap/Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/index.html#det-catalan-type-hankel-eq-zero"}],"result_edges":[["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/coeff-hermite-shift-mul-trinomial-neg-po","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/kampe-de-feriet-hermite",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/kampe-de-feriet-hermite-two-two","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/kampe-de-feriet-hermite",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/kampe-de-feriet-hermite-zero","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/kampe-de-feriet-hermite",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/coeff-catalan-type-kernel-decomposition","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/coeff-hermite-shift-mul-trinomial-neg-po","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/coeff-nieto-hermite-series-mul-trinomial","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/constant-coeff-trinomial-neg-pow-series","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/det-catalan-type-hankel-eq-zero","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/det-catalan-type-lower-triangular","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0],["Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series-neg-one","Algebra/orthogonal-polynomials/catalan-type-polynomials/trinomial-neg-pow-series",1,0]],"boundary_edges":[],"outside":[]},"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups":{"part":{"id":"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups","title":"3-transposition groups","results":4,"status":{"can_prove":1,"fully_proved":3},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":0,"file":"52.json"},"path":[["region:Algebra & Number Theory","Algebra & Number Theory"],["GroupTheory","Group Theory"],["GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups","Finite simple groups and involutions"],["GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups","3-transposition groups"]],"children":[],"edges":[],"links":{"uses":[],"uses_total":0,"used_by":[],"used_by_total":0},"results":[{"id":"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/is-three-transposition-set","title":"Set of 3-transpositions","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted.IsThreeTranspositionSet","summary":"A subset $D$ of a group $G$ is a set of 3-transpositions when it is closed under conjugation ($g d g^{-1} \\in D$ for all $g \\in G$ and $d \\in D$), every $d \\in D$ is an involution ($d \\ne 1$ and $d^2 = 1$), and for all $d, e \\in D$ the product $de$ has order at most $3$, that is, $de = 1$, $(de)^2 = 1$ or $(de)^3 = 1$.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/index.html#is-three-transposition-set"},{"id":"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/is-locally-finite","title":"Locally finite group, via finite subsets","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted.IsLocallyFinite","summary":"A group $G$ is locally finite when every finite subset $S \\subseteq G$ generates a finite subgroup; equivalently, every finitely generated subgroup of $G$ is finite.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/index.html#is-locally-finite"},{"id":"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite","title":"Sozutov's question on 3-transposition groups (Kourovka 12.76)","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted.threeTranspositionGroupLocallyFinite","summary":"The statement that for every group $G$ and every set $D$ of 3-transpositions of $G$ that generates $G$, the group $G$ is locally finite.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/index.html#three-transposition-group-locally-finite"},{"id":"GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite-2","title":"Groups generated by 3-transpositions are locally finite","status":"can_prove","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ThreeTranspositionLocallyFiniteKourovka12Problem76Wanted.threeTranspositionGroupLocallyFinite_holds","summary":"If a group $G$ is generated by a set $D$ of 3-transpositions (a conjugation-invariant set of involutions whose pairwise products have order at most $3$), then $G$ is locally finite: every finite subset of $G$ generates a finite subgroup of $G$.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/index.html#three-transposition-group-locally-finite-2"}],"result_edges":[["GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite","GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/is-locally-finite",1,0],["GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite","GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/is-three-transposition-set",1,0],["GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite-2","GroupTheory/finite-simple-groups/3-transposition-groups/three-transposition-group-locally-finite",1,0]],"boundary_edges":[],"outside":[]},"GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups":{"part":{"id":"GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups","title":"Cofinitary permutation groups","results":3,"status":{"can_prove":1,"fully_proved":2},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":1,"file":"52.json"},"path":[["region:Algebra & Number Theory","Algebra & Number Theory"],["GroupTheory","Group Theory"],["GroupTheory/permutation-groups","Permutation groups and group actions"],["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups","Infinite permutation groups"],["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups","Cofinitary permutation groups"]],"children":[],"edges":[],"links":{"uses":[["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/highly-homogeneous-permutation-groups","Highly homogeneous permutation groups","Infinite permutation groups",1]],"uses_total":1,"used_by":[],"used_by_total":0},"results":[{"id":"GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/is-cofinitary","title":"Cofinitary permutation groups","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ClosedCofinitaryLocallyCompactKourovka13Problem28Wanted.IsCofinitary","summary":"A subgroup $G$ of the group of permutations of a set $\\Omega$ is cofinitary when every non-identity element of $G$ fixes only finitely many points of $\\Omega$.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/index.html#is-cofinitary"},{"id":"GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact","title":"Claim of Kourovka Problem 13.28","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ClosedCofinitaryLocallyCompactKourovka13Problem28Wanted.closedCofinitaryLocallyCompact","summary":"For every infinite set $\\Omega$, every cofinitary subgroup $G$ of the group of permutations of $\\Omega$ that is closed in the topology of pointwise convergence is locally compact in the subspace topology.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/index.html#closed-cofinitary-locally-compact"},{"id":"GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact-refute","title":"Closed cofinitary permutation groups need not be locally compact","status":"can_prove","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MathlibExt.GroupTheory.ClosedCofinitaryLocallyCompactKourovka13Problem28Wanted.closedCofinitaryLocallyCompact_refuted","summary":"The claim of Kourovka Problem 13.28 is false: there are an infinite set $\\Omega$ and a subgroup $G$ of the group of permutations of $\\Omega$ such that $G$ is cofinitary (every non-identity element of $G$ fixes only finitely many points of $\\Omega$) and closed in the topology of pointwise convergence, but $G$ is not locally compact in the subspace topology.","route":"../roadmap/GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/index.html#closed-cofinitary-locally-compact-refute"}],"result_edges":[["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact-refute","GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact",1,0],["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact","GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/is-cofinitary",1,0]],"boundary_edges":[["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/cofinitary-permutation-groups/closed-cofinitary-locally-compact","GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/highly-homogeneous-permutation-groups/pointwise-topology",1,0]],"outside":[["GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/highly-homogeneous-permutation-groups/pointwise-topology","Pointwise convergence topology on permutations",["region:Algebra & Number Theory","GroupTheory","GroupTheory/permutation-groups","GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups","GroupTheory/permutation-groups/infinite-permutation-groups/highly-homogeneous-permutation-groups"]]]},"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups":{"part":{"id":"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups","title":"Automorphism groups","results":29,"status":{"can_prove":13,"fully_proved":16},"parts":8,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":0,"file":"52.json"},"path":[["region:Algebra & Number Theory","Algebra & Number Theory"],["GroupTheory","Group Theory"],["GroupTheory/automorphisms","Automorphisms of groups"],["GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups","Automorphism groups"]],"children":[{"id":"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups/automorphism-groups-of-p-groups","title":"Automorphism groups of p-groups","results":2,"status":{"can_prove":1,"fully_proved":1},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":0,"file":"06.json"},{"id":"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups/class-preserving-automorphisms","title":"Class-preserving automorphisms","results":4,"status":{"can_prove":2,"fully_proved":2},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":0,"file":"56.json"},{"id":"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups/endomorphisms-and-partial-isomorphisms","title":"Endomorphisms and partial isomorphisms","results":2,"status":{"can_prove":1,"fully_proved":1},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_total":0,"file":"b4.json"},{"id":"GroupTheory/automorphisms/automorphism-groups/groups-as-automorphism-groups-of-relations","title":"Groups as automorphism groups of relations","results":2,"status":{"can_prove":1,"fully_proved":1},"parts":0,"api":[],"api_total":0,"imports_to