 complete graph $K_n$, whose vertices are the natural numbers less than $n$, $x$ and $y$ are adjacent if and only if $x \\ne y$.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#clique-fin-graph-adj"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/minor-step","title":"One-step graph minor","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Inductive","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.MinorStep","summary":"For finite graphs $H$ and $G$, $H$ arises from $G$ by one minor step when one of the following holds. Isomorphism: $H$ is isomorphic to $G$. Vertex deletion: for some vertex $a$ of $G$ there is an injective map $f$ from the vertices of $H$ to the vertices of $G$ whose image consists of all vertices other than $a$, such that vertices $u, v$ of $H$ are adjacent exactly when $f(u), f(v)$ are adjacent in $G$. Edge…","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#minor-step"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor","title":"Graph minor relation","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Definition","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsMinor","summary":"For finite graphs $H$ and $G$, $H$ is a minor of $G$ when there is a finite sequence $H = G_0, G_1, \\dots, G_k = G$ of finite graphs, with $k \\ge 0$, such that for each $i < k$, $G_i$ arises from $G_{i+1}$ by one minor step: an isomorphism, the deletion of one vertex, the deletion of one edge, or the contraction of one edge.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#is-minor"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/refl","title":"Reflexivity of the graph minor relation","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsMinor.refl","summary":"Every finite graph $G$ is a minor of itself.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#refl"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/trans","title":"Transitivity of the graph minor relation","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsMinor.trans","summary":"For finite graphs $H$, $K$ and $G$, if $H$ is a minor of $K$ and $K$ is a minor of $G$, then $H$ is a minor of $G$.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#trans"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-step","title":"A single minor step gives a minor","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsMinor.of_step","summary":"For finite graphs $H$ and $G$, if $H$ arises from $G$ by one minor step, then $H$ is a minor of $G$.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#of-step"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-iso","title":"An isomorphic copy is a minor","status":"fully_proved","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.IsMinor.of_iso","summary":"For finite graphs $H$ and $G$, if $H$ is isomorphic to $G$, then $H$ is a minor of $G$.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#of-iso"},{"id":"GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/robertson-seymour","title":"Robertson–Seymour theorem","status":"can_prove","caption":"Theorem","lean":"MetaMathlibExt.robertson_seymour","summary":"Let $F$ be a family of finite simple graphs that is closed under taking minors: whenever $G \\in F$ and $H$ is a minor of $G$, also $H \\in F$. Then any two finite graphs that are minors of each other are isomorphic, and there is a finite set $O$ of finite graphs such that a finite graph $G$ belongs to $F$ if and only if no graph in $O$ is a minor of $G$.","route":"../roadmap/GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/index.html#robertson-seymour"}],"result_edges":[["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/clique-fin-graph-adj","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/clique-fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/clique-fin-graph","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/clique-fin-graph-adj","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/minor-step","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-iso","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-step","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/refl","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/robertson-seymour","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/trans","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/fin-graph",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-iso","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-step","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/refl","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/robertson-seymour","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/trans","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/is-minor","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/minor-step",1,0],["GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/of-iso","GraphTheory/planar-graphs-and-minors/graph-minors/minor-step",0,1],["GraphTheory/pl